1 SPAWN
    SPAWN creates a subprocess of the console's process, as DCL's SPAWN and
    a program's LIB$SPAWN do, and (unless /NOWAIT) waits for it to end.  It
    needs vax.process.scheduler on, as it is unless the configuration turns
    it off (see HELP CONFIG KEYS).

    Syntax:

        SPAWN [/NOWAIT] [/INPUT=file] [/OUTPUT=file] [/PROCESS=name]
              [/PROMPT=string] [/NOSYMBOLS] [/NOLOGICAL_NAMES] [command]

    The subprocess runs DCL, the same command interpreter as the console:
    it runs the command and logs out, or, with no command, reads commands
    from its input until the end of the input, LOGOUT, or EXIT.  Every DCL
    command works there, command procedures (@) included, with the
    subprocess's own symbols (copies of the console's, unless /NOSYMBOLS),
    logical names (copies of its process logical names, unless
    /NOLOGICAL_NAMES), default directory, $STATUS, and open files.  The
    commands that work the machine itself (DEBUG, ASM, INITIALIZE/VAX,
    VMINIT, ZERO, SAVE, LOAD, ...) are the console's own, and give
    %VAX-E-NOTSUBPROC.  A subprocess can SPAWN a subprocess of its own,
    which it waits for (unless /NOWAIT) as the console does; a process's
    subprocesses end when it does.  SPAWN says when the subprocess has
    been made (%DCL-S-SPAWNED, and %DCL-S-ATTACHED if it waits) and when
    control is back (%DCL-S-RETURNED), as DCL does.  SPAWN's status is
    the subprocess's final status.

        /NOWAIT         Don't wait: the subprocess runs beside the console.
                        It takes turns with the console's DCL, between its
                        commands (one command each, in a procedure), during
                        WAIT, while the machine runs (RUN, GO, CALL), and
                        while the VAX> prompt waits for a line.  What it
                        writes meanwhile appears above the line being
                        typed, which is shown again below it.  (With input
                        from a pipe, not a terminal, it runs at the prompt
                        only until it waits or has run a while.)
        /INPUT=file     The subprocess's SYS$INPUT: the terminal, a mailbox,
                        or a file of commands.  Default: the console's.
        /OUTPUT=file    Its SYS$OUTPUT: a terminal, a mailbox, NL:, or a
                        file, which gets each line as a record.
        /PROCESS=name   Its name.  Default: the user name and "_n".
        /PROMPT=string  The prompt for commands read from the terminal.
                        Default: "$ ".

    Example:

        VAX> HELLO :== $DUA0:[WORK]HELLO.EXE
        VAX> SPAWN HELLO
        VAX> SPAWN DIRECTORY
        VAX> SPAWN/NOWAIT @NIGHTLY

    A SPAWN that waits gives the subprocess the terminal.  ATTACH gives it
    back, or to another process of the console's job (see HELP ATTACH).
